		 <td>                            
	                                   

<P>The house was not as it should be.</P><br><P>Furniture was overturned, and papers and books—her precious papers and books—were strewn wildly about the floor.</P><br><P>My hand fell to the hilt of my sword, and the pelts I had ben carrying in my arms fell to the floor.</P><br><P>&#9;Layla hung by her wrists, tied to a crossbeam in the ceiling. Her dress was in tatters and she was covered in blood from the long, deep razor cuts on her arms…..and her face. There was a gag in her mouth…or…rather…what was left of her mouth—to silence her screams. The flesh of her hands was black where the ropes bound her. She must have been hanging there all day.</P><br><P>&#9;Her, swollen, beaten eyes opened slightly, and the light of recognition lit in them.</P><br><P>&#9;It felt as though the soul had been drained out of me…all the world was cold and frost. The bile rose in my throat, and the tears rose in my eyes.</P><br><P>&#9;A feeling of utter helplessless like I have never felt…and will never feel again, swept over me. However long she had been here…like this…I had not known…and could not help. </P><br><P>&#9;Helpless….</P><br><P>&#9;Instanly I was torn between two overpowering urges. First to get her down from there. And second—to destroy whatever monster (for it could be no man) that had done this thing.</P><br><P>&#9;The only piece of furniture left standing in the room was our chair. The same chair the only a moment before I had imagined Layla and I nested in for the night. It was a wide, high-backed chair, that we normally kept in front of the glass window. Now, however, it was facing away from the door—pulled into the center of the room as a single seat for a madman’s private show.</P><br><P>&#9;The figure in the chair rose and tunred to face me.</P><br><P>&#9;It was Hager Sureshot.</P><br><P>&#9;He looked at me for a moment, or perhaps it was a month. There were spatters of blood on his face and scratch marks over his eyes that must have been made by Layla, in her desperate defense. In one hand, he held a cup of mead from our kitchen. In the other, my shaving razor…covered in Layla’s blood. He cocked his head sideways in a curious gaze, and a slight smile touched his mouth.</P><br><P>&#9;When he spoke, his voice was not the gruff, terse tone of Hager Sureshot, but the delicate musical sound of my Layla. He…or it…said my name. </P><br><P>&#9;"Eshkin…my love…where are you?"</P><br><P>&#9;My sword whistled from its scabbard in a blur and my entire being erupted in invincible rage.</P><br><P>Had I known the name if Innoruk then, I would have forsaken Surefall, forsaken Tunare, and called on the Prince of Hate, for the power to crush this thing bare-handed—to dine on its screams and lavish in the feeling of tearing the flesh from its bones.</P><br><P>The shock of my own blow ran up my arm as the blade choped into the flesh of its neck. </P><br><P>And it smiled.</P><br><P>The blow that impacted against the side of my head was mericful. Blood exploded in my sinuses and warm, white light flashed before my eyes.</P><br><P>I don’t remember much of the fight—save that it was hopless. My last recollection of it was that Sureshot’s arms had changed—growing into disproportionate claws….like the blades of a scythe. They scissored in on my forearms, cutting deep, holding me helpless off the floor, like a bloody marionette.</P><br><P>It’s held me up to her; to the tortured, mutilated face of my love.</P><br><P>Again, it spoke in her vioce, "Eshkin…kiss me, my love…we can’t be late for dinner…"</P><br><P>I screamed then, I think…and it laughed. Hager’s body, Layla’s voice…</P><br><P>Through my screams and tears I wished for death.</P><br><P>"Hmmm…delicious…" it said.</P><br><P>And, as a child might skip a stone over the water, it cast me out the window. The strength of its throw, as it propelled me, was unearthly. I remember the shattering of Erudite glass, and the cuts it dealt me, only vaguely. I remember the fall, and the feeling of weightlesness, and a dream of pain, of blood erupting up into my mouth…and of bones splintering like glass. Was it an hour, or only a moment, I do not recall—but my blood-blind eyes opened to find me on the roof of the Rangers’ cabin in the Glade. </P><br><P>My body had made a dent in the roof.</P><br><P>Broken ribs, legs, fingers…everything was a dull haze of pain. Everything was growing cold…and my body began to shake. Death would come soon…only a moment.</P><br><P>From the roof of the cabin I could see the very top gables of my house above….and the creature.</P><br><P>It had cast off all pretense, and was now not even vaguely human.</P><br><P>It had climbed to the roof, with my Layla cradled in its claws. It must have unbound her mouth, because I could hear her screams…and I remembered it’s word…</P><br><I><P>Delicious</I>.</P><br><P>Agony…fear…sorrow. It loved these things.</P><br><P>Layla was terribly fearful of heights…and so it would finish her…by casting her down into the glade.</P><br><P>NO.</P><br><P>Though my legs did not work, somehow my arms did. I managed to roll onto my side and pull my bow from its quiver. My bloodied hand shakily felt for the one thing I needed…a last hope.</P><br><P>The black arrow.</P><br><P>Laying in anguish I fitted it to the nock and drew with all my strength. I could hear the fiber of the bow splintering from within, from the force, as I drew.</P><br><P>One shot…one hope…with the black arrow that had never failed me.</P><br><P>I missed.</P><br><P>I closed my eyes and relaxed into death as I heard my love’s screams silenced against the stone.</P><br><P>I would be with her soon.</P><br><P>Layla…I come.</P>         <br>
